{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And to the nobles, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say ״וְאֶל אֲצִילֵי״ and it was not sufficient to say ״וְלֹא שָׁלַח בָּהֶם יָדוֹ״, and the matter is obvious that it is speaking of the elders, etc., who are mentioned nearby. Or perhaps it is giving the reason why He did not stretch forth His hand — because they were אֲצִילֵי, meaning the great ones of Israel, and had others done so He would have stretched forth His hand. Or according to what they of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 15) that He did not wish to disrupt the joy, for all Israel would have mourned over them because they were their great ones and their nobles; therefore He did not stretch forth His hand." "<b>He did not stretch forth, etc., and they ate, etc.</b> It is difficult why it repeated a second time <b>and they beheld</b>, etc., and was not satisfied with what preceded, when it said ״וַיִּרְאוּ אֵת״ etc.? Now our Rabbis of blessed memory (Leviticus Rabbah 20:10) said that the intention is to say that through the vision of Shaddai they enjoyed and felt health and satiety as one who has eaten and drunk. Yet it is still difficult why the verse interrupted in the middle of the matter with the statement ״לֹא שָׁלַח יָדוֹ״, for it should have been proper to first state their actions in fullness and afterward the slowness of anger that HaShem extended to them and did not kill them immediately, in this manner: ״וַיִּרְאוּ אֵת אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc. ״וַיֶּחֱזוּ אֶת הָאֱלֹהִים וַיֹּאכְלוּ״ etc. ״וְאֶל אֲצִילֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״. And also there would have been no need to write a second time ״וַיֶּחֱזוּ״ etc., and it would have been sufficient with what it began to say, ״וַיִּרְאוּ אֶת אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc. ״לָטֹהַר וַיֹּאכְלוּ״ etc., and the exposition of our Sages of blessed memory does not move from its place." "<b>Or perhaps</b> the intention of the verse follows what He said (Exodus 33:23) ״וַהֲסִירֹתִי אֶת כַּפִּי וְרָאִיתָ״ etc. — see, then, that YHWH makes a barrier with His hand for the created beings from looking upon the Divine, and even to Moses when He revealed Himself to him in a revelation of the Shekhinah He placed His hand upon him — meaning, an aspect that prevents looking — and here the verse informs us that concerning the nobles of Israel He did not stretch forth His hand to withhold the vision from them, because they were esteemed in His eyes, and He left them to nourish their eyes from His light, blessed be He." "And this is the measurement of the verse: <b>and they saw the Elohim of Israel</b> — meaning, they saw a great light of the Elohim of Israel, but they did not gaze except under His feet. And afterward, because HaShem did not stretch forth His hand — meaning, His power and permission to withhold from them — through this they had the strength to gaze upon the pleasantness of YHWH, and they enjoyed — this is eating, this is drinking — and their souls were sated with goodness." "And it should not be difficult why to Moses He placed His hand upon him and prevented him from looking, while to the nobles of Israel He did not stretch forth His hand — for what required HaShem to place His hand upon Moses was not to deprive him of the perception that the nobles of Israel attained, for Moses had this perception constantly, but rather to look upon a more exalted perception, the vision of every supernal vision. And there are yet more things to Elohim in the explanation of these matters, and I guard myself from setting down in a book things that dwell in the secret of the Most High, lest they benefit from them one who is not worthy to shine in the light of life." "<b>Thus is concluded the parashah of Mishpatim.</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {5 "This is a standard colophon marking the end of the Torah portion; חסלת is an Aramaic formula meaning 'is completed.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:33:27.948568772Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}